{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head 8f85a0d5fa0766671089055edd4fb3dbe2d78d7b, covering the sole artifact notes/2026-10-01-artroom-plan.md.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: one authoritative Room, HTTPS acts, fork-scoped writes and immutable proposed-head pinning form a sound starting point. The plan needs explicit approval, landing and recovery invariants before it can guide implementation.\nSecurity conclusion: signatures and fork tokens are necessary but do not establish permission to review, satisfy checks, change policy or land. Define these trust boundaries in the contract.\nSimplification conclusion: preserve the typed API, intent visibility and attention queue. Reduce the competition implementation to one complete, dependable workflow; the current cut list barely reduces the critical path.\n\nRequired plan revisions\n\n1. P1 — Approval reuse must describe its limits and protect the resulting candidate (lines 152–161, 301–306, 431–440, 517–520).\nAn unchanged login handler can become unsafe when a different file changes its imported authorization helper. Path equality establishes byte stability, not continued truth of a review. Likewise a passing test result cannot survive arbitrary changes outside the reviewed paths.\nKeep every original verdict attached to its original head. Specify when policy may reuse it as evidence for a newer proposal, show the source head and reason, and separate reviewed scope from declared dependencies. Use conservative invalidation for dependency manifests, build configuration, policy and other global inputs. Required machine checks should bind to the exact resulting integration tree or commit and checker configuration. Rerun them after integration changes unless a documented input-equivalence rule applies. Add an unchanged-file/changed-dependency example to the acceptance criteria. Replace the promise that verdicts last exactly as long as they are true with an honest statement of the reuse rule.\n\n2. P1 — Parallel preparation needs guarded publication and crash recovery (lines 397–425, 434, 499–504).\nTwo disjoint changes prepared against main M still compete to update the same ref. Publishing one candidate must preserve the other change, and the candidate checked before publication must be the candidate that lands. A single Durable Object also allows request interleaving while external I/O is awaited; it is not a transaction across SQLite and Git. Cloudflare documents this behavior in its Durable Object guidance: https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/rules-of-durable-objects/.\nDescribe parallel previews/checks with serialized or batched guarded updates to main. Record a durable operation ID, expected main, proposal generation, policy version, integration candidate and supporting evidence; compare-and-swap main; reconcile the durable operation after a timeout or restart before attempting another publication. Distinguish accepted land intent, pending work, confirmed landing and retryable failure. Include crashes before push, after successful push but before the SQLite receipt, and a policy/release/main change during a preview. The published log should expose its committed high-water mark and publication lag; offline verification proves a published prefix, not completeness of unpublished state. Lane sharding is a future design question because main, policy and cross-lane dependencies remain shared.\n\n3. P1 — Define authorization, policy activation and execution boundaries (lines 167–178, 291–338, 344–381, 504–507).\nA claimant must not be able to satisfy its own required test merely by signing a check. Bind each obligation to authorized identities or delegations, the exact proposal generation and the relevant configuration. Define room membership, role administration, invitation expiry, key revocation and delegation limits. Sign a versioned canonical envelope that binds room, actor, act kind, target and idempotency key; reject reuse of one key for a different payload. Actor identity must come from verified credentials, rather than trusting the API's as field.\nEvaluate proposal ownership and requirements from actual changed paths, including old and new rename paths; a declared claim scope cannot conceal a sensitive change. Document bounded glob intersection for claims about files that do not yet exist. A policy-changing proposal must be authorized under the currently active policy, with a fixed administration/recovery boundary. Explain what happens to open proposals and obligations at activation.\nLease renewal, expiry and handover need an explicit method or system event, generation fencing and revocation of the previous workspace token. Expiry can preserve existing context; it cannot create a handover note that a dead agent never wrote.\nKeep the merge sandbox separate from untrusted npm scripts and check execution. Untrusted execution must never receive canonical write credentials or room signing keys. Fetch the pinned candidate, verify HEAD and give the runner only the necessary read access. The shallow default-branch clone in the example does not guarantee that the proposed head exists locally.\nPreserve atseq's actual evaluator budgets. Its current src/runtime/evaluator.ts and docs/runtime-profile.md already bound AST depth/size, evaluator visits, intermediate sequences and inspected bytes; line 585's description omits those protections. Pin the portable profile and interpreter version, port the conformance corpus, and define deterministic refusal versus runtime failure. The Node-specific dependency integrity adapter needs a Workers-compatible implementation. The Worker CPU limit alone cannot provide a replayable domain outcome.\n\n4. P1 — Resolve the secret-redaction contradiction (lines 167–168, 402–404, 503).\nA plaintext signed note published in a clonable immutable Git log remains recoverable when the UI hides it. The spike README explicitly identifies this limitation. Specify a consistent MVP: reject detected secrets before durable publication, state that detection is incomplete, and document credential rotation for a missed secret. If body removal is required, retain only a commitment in the immutable log and store the removable body separately, with explicit offline-verification limits. Do not promise that a published secret can be hidden from every view.\n\n5. P1 — Rework scope and capacity before committing to dates (lines 538–588).\nThe listed lanes sum to 28.5 engineer-days, not 29.5. Multiple lanes do not supply multiple staffed builders, and the plan names no capacity or independent review/integration allowance. The contract, Git integration, authorization, checker isolation, publication recovery and UI converge on the same end-to-end path. A ±4-day range is unsupported for these unproven integrations.\nName the available builders, planner/reviewer capacity, dependency order and explicit integration reserve. Set an early deployed vertical-slice gate: claim, fork, push, pin, review/check, safe land, attention and verify. State the date and observable result that triggers cuts.\nMy recommended competition baseline is one repository per room, restricted policy data, two agent clients, one check runner and a small Room/Proposal/Needs-you interface. Stage OAuth, package scaffolding, the separate Policy screen, automatic line re-anchoring, alternative runtimes and the conventional-workflow simulator after that slice. Keep the API and policy extension contracts while shipping one implementation path. Put correctness edge cases into the owning lanes rather than saving them for lane I. Treat container fallback as a separately tested scope restriction, not a free replacement.\n\n6. P1 — The API contract must include the transitions the demo depends on (lines 212–287, 363–381, 468–471).\nThe seven-act vocabulary is a useful core. Before other lanes depend on api.d.ts, define stable act/room/lane/proposal IDs; explicit immutable head and expected generation; the meaning of propose on an existing lane; scope changes; claim leases and renewal; release and handover; trusted check submission; asynchronous preview/workspace/landing states; conflict recut ownership; and bounded attention/log pagination with resumable cursors. Claim overlap should be available immediately, while conflict previews can return pending.\nSpecify the actual credential and signing flows for browser, HTTPS, MCP and service-binding callers. Keep transport errors distinguishable from policy refusals, and handle Refusal at each dependent call in the example rather than only at claim. A disposable room handle should have a defined cleanup obligation; a resumable attention feed should be a documented subscription/pagination contract, not an assumed serializable async iterator across every transport. Show how an agent obtains its workspace and renews/releases its lease through MCP; the listed five tools alone do not cover the demonstrated loop.\nThis is contract-level work; the requested plan revision does not need implementation code.\n\n7. P2 — Qualify evidence and product claims (lines 86–104, 142–162, 423–425, 482–490, 525–529).\nThe spike at 58dbdbf measured a SHA-256 digest and one SQLite insert, using 32 closed-loop clients. It did not exercise signature verification, policy, obligations, workspace provisioning, diffs or publication. Retain 132 ms and 201 appends/s as baseline observations, and label full-system targets as unmeasured. A 100-agent claim benchmark is distinct from 100 concurrent coding/review/landing workflows. Publish workload size, geography, sample counts, p99 measurement, retries, cold/warm state, fork provisioning time and Artifacts/container cost.\nTree reads return immediate children, so large-tree traversal and caching need a bound; the binding has no automatic semantic conflict detector. Official APIs: https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/api/workers-binding/. Include the namespace-wide control-plane limit and storage/blob limits as well as the per-repository Git limit: https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/platform/limits/.\nTreat zero wasted approvals as a target for a controlled serialized-recut scenario, with reviewer waiting and throughput reported too. Label the PR comparison as a model; it does not measure GitHub performance. The 84-event and 54%-changes-requested statistics require denominators and event categories and do not by themselves prove ceremony caused that cost.\nThe cited study's 41.7% and 19.8% describe replayed textual conflicts among sampled co-active pairs, not all agent PRs; cross-agent pairs were rare. Preserve that qualification: https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04697.\nReplace the universal market claim \"What no one offers\" with specific supported comparisons and Artroom's intended combination. The announcement supports Workers/Artifacts, concurrent agents, source, video and run instructions: https://blog.cloudflare.com/next-git-platform-on-cloudflare/. I could not independently retrieve the competition-terms PDF, so I have not verified its exact cutoff time, scoring or attendance terms.\n\nViews on section 13\n\n1. Policy language: use the bounded JSONata data profile with TypeScript helpers. Keep authorization and persistence safety invariants in platform code. Arbitrary TypeScript policy execution is beyond the competition baseline.\n2. Runtime reuse: reuse the isolated portable atseq evaluator and admission checks with pinned dependencies and conformance vectors. Avoid importing the entire application runtime or its Node-only adapters.\n3. Log format: use a versioned Artroom format unless existing gitseq primitives can be reused cheaply. Define canonical bytes, room genesis, actor membership, signatures, sequence/hash chain, retained policy inputs, system receipts and checkpoints before promising offline verification. Do not require byte compatibility merely for gs verify.\n4. Hosting: start on workers.dev in an account confirmed to support Artifacts and Sandbox, with a quota-limited demo room. Domain setup can follow. Account ownership and deployment authority remain planner/user decisions.\n5. Actors: I am acting as checker for independent exact-head review. Start with at most two implementation lanes until the deployed vertical slice passes; increase only when independent work and integration capacity are demonstrated. This is a staffing recommendation, not an assignment of additional agents.\n6. Packages: use an owned scope initially. The npm name artroom has not been checked; package availability should not gate the implementation.\n\nValidation: read the entire exact-head note, its request/promise/artifact chain, the spike README and harness at 58dbdbf, the reused evaluator/profile, and the linked primary sources above. Documentation review only; no application tests or deployment were performed. The next plan head should resolve items 1–6 with explicit invariants and scope decisions, and correct or qualify item 7. No source files changed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0b29c4228ba96be2e989dd1a883f520ef1520882","binding":"assigned","head":"8f85a0d5fa0766671089055edd4fb3dbe2d78d7b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2e5b37da635f3e3d53cf09ab08adee9628454b7e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2bb1a40df6c55cf3c724fd05fe6ac9ece18472c1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:19a3270303ea86c5b9fe14059782f8d48b518020","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}